  "body": "## Summary\n\n`dag-lint` and `dag-rounds` gave a **false failing verdict on a valid external plan**, and the way they failed cost the operator work rather than stopping them.\n\n`derived_repo_root` named a repository by position:\n\n```python\nif plan_path.parent.name == \".autopilot\":\n    return plan_path.parent.parent\nreturn plan_path.parent          # <- the plan's own folder\n```\n\nA plan installed at `<root>/.autopilot/plan.json` resolves correctly. A plan filed by its subject, e.g. `Brain/plans/dags/<subject>/plan.json`, resolved to `Brain/plans/dags/<subject>/`, a directory containing none of the repository.\n\nEvery path-existence check then ran against that root. The scaffold-collision check judges only scaffolds that do not yet exist (`dag_standard.py:2015-2032`), so under an empty root each **established** directory read as a first-time creation contested by whichever nodes write into it.\n\nMeasured on a real 13-node external plan: **3 errors and 2 warnings**, naming `Brain/reviews/receipts/` as contested by three nodes when it already holds 517 tracked entries, plus two package `__init__.py` files that are already committed. Resolved to the actual repository, the same plan lints **0 errors, 0 warnings, exit 0**.\n\nThe failure direction is what makes this worth fixing rather than documenting. It does not crash and it does not return nothing. It returns specific, credible, actionable findings that are entirely false.\n\n`effective_repo_root` already refuses a declared `--repo-root` the plan does not live inside and delegates to `derived_repo_root`, so the intended safety net was reached and then gave the wrong answer. A subject's `run.sh` wrapper only works because it sidesteps the bug, staging the plan at `<repo-root>/.autopilot/plan.json` so the fast path fires.\n\n**Fix:** walk up from the plan's directory to the nearest ancestor carrying `.git`, counting both a directory (normal clone) and a file (linked worktree). Fall back to the previous behavior when no ancestor carries one, preserving the synthetic and pre-clone plan case the callers already handle. The `.autopilot/plan.json` fast path is untouched.\n\n## Governance tier\n\n- [ ] Governance-lite\n- [x] **Governed**\n\nIt changes executable behavior in the authoring standard, so it cannot use governance-lite.\n\nEvidence, as an outside contribution: the defect and the verdict flip are reproducible from the description above against any plan filed outside `.autopilot/`, and the new tests encode the invariant permanently. I have not produced a courtroom disposition, an ADR, or eight-role evidence, since those are maintainer instruments; please tell me what this change needs and I will add it.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `.autopilot/tests/test_dag_standard.py` — **111 tests, OK**, including 5 new cases in `ExternalPlanRepoRootTests`.\n- The new tests **bite rather than assert the fix.** One pair holds the plan and its nodes fixed and varies only whether an ancestor carries `.git`: the collision is reported without a repository and absent with one. Silencing the check instead of fixing the resolver fails the suite. The other three pin the linked-worktree `.git`-as-file shape, the `.autopilot/plan.json` fast path, and the resolved root itself.\n- Full `.autopilot/tests` tree: 452 tests, 7 failures + 2 errors. **All pre-existing and unrelated.** They live in `test_parallel_worktree_execution`, `test_post_merge_repair` and `test_explorer_receipt_retirement`, none of which imports `dag_standard`, and every one reproduces standalone in a process where `test_dag_standard` is never imported. They are git/worktree/remote operations failing in this sandbox, and they are not the `GIT_EDITOR` trap documented in `DAG_EXECUTION_HANDOFF.md` §6: they still fail with every `GIT_*` variable stripped.\n- `ruff check` and `ruff format`: no new finding in the added lines. The repo carries one pre-existing `I001` on `dag_standard.py` and pre-existing format drift in unrelated regions; I left both alone rather than reformat ~390 lines of untouched code inside a 30-line fix.\n- `pyright`: 0 errors in both changed regions. The 19 reported on the test file are pre-existing `Import \"autopilot\" could not be resolved` at lines 1866/1911/1970/2005.\n- Not run: the repo-wide `tests/` gate. Only `tests/test_generic_dag_v3_overlay.py` references `dag_standard`, and `src/` is untouched, so per §6 of the handoff the leased full run is not warranted for this diff. Say the word if you want it anyway.\n\n## Safety\n\n- [x] No credentials, private data, or unrelated generated artifacts are included.\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md).\n\nTwo files changed, both under `.autopilot/`. No `src/`, no `evidence/`, no `.github/`, no plan or policy data.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01QYona8m9SKg1TvxD42VynF)_",
